  "worked_solution": "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{\u2032}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\frac{1}{3}{({x}^{2}-2)}^{3/2},$ $a=2,$ and $b=5.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{\u2032}(x)=\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}{({x}^{2}-2)}^{1/2}\\cdot 2x=x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-2}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{\u2032}(x)]^{2}}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{\u2032}(x)]^{2}} & =\\sqrt{1+{[x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-2}]}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}({x}^{2}-2)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}-2{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}^{2}-1)^{2}} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{\u2032}(x)]^{2}}={x}^{2}-1$ to the formula for the arc length, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{2}^{5}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{\u2032}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{5}({x}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}-x)\\,{|}_{2}^{5} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{3}(5)^{3}-5]-[\\frac{1}{3}(2)^{3}-2] \\\\  & =\\frac{110}{3}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
